{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>This is the thing that HaShem has commanded: Gather of it, every man according to his eating.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: Moses said to them — do not say that perhaps Nachshon son of Amminadav and all the mighty men will gather much, while a poor man of Israel comes and gathers little; therefore it says 'every man according to his eating.' And how much was 'according to his eating'? An omer per head (גלגלת). For if one eats this amount, he is healthy; less than this, his intestines are disordered; more than this, he is a glutton. And what it says, 'the number of persons, every man according to those in his tent, you shall take' — 'those in his tent' means his wife, who is worthy to be found in the tent, in the manner of what is written (Genesis 18:9) 'in the tent,' and it is written (Psalms 45:14) 'All glorious is the king's daughter within' etc. And Scripture teaches by way of asmakhta that a man is obligated in the sustenance (מזונות) of his wife and his small children, and this is what it says, 'the number of your persons.' Thus explained our Rabbi Chananel of blessed memory." "<b>And know</b> that this verse embraces two great principles, each of which embraces both the supernal beings and the lower ones, and they are: the letters and sustenance (פרנסה). For there is nothing in the world that is not encompassed in the forms of the 22 letters, and likewise there is nothing found in the world, whether among the supernal beings or the lower ones, that does not require sustenance — whoever needs continued existence from another, that is his sustenance. Our Rabbis of blessed memory already explained to us the virtue of the psalm 'A praise of David' (תהלה לדוד), that one who says it three times every day is assured that he is a son of the World to Come, and they revealed to us the essential reason: because it encompasses the letters and sustenance. The letters — it is arranged in alphabetical order (אלפא ביתא), and sustenance — it is written in it (Psalms 145:16) 'You open Your hand' etc. Now this verse embraces both of them, for the 22 letters of the Torah are inscribed in it, and it speaks of the matter of sustenance. From this our Rabbis of blessed memory derived: the Torah was given only to the eaters of the manna. And there is a tradition in the hands of the Sages that whoever recites the section of the manna every day is assured that he will not come into lack of food in this world."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:00:09.880153242Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}